From 7bd850b8d7f105dc18217f1d3fb712b9c7020044 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 10:10:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] The rack, the look engine, and a layout that fits every
 screen
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Audio (js/audio.js, js/pitch-worklet.js)
  A real signal chain between the file and the speakers: preamp, ten-band
  parametric EQ, bass and treble shelves, compressor, convolution reverb
  built from noise rather than shipped samples, stereo width, balance and a
  brickwall limiter — plus pitch and speed as two separate controls.

  Pitch is the interesting one. `playbackRate` moves pitch and tempo
  together, because that is what spinning a record faster does. Speed here
  uses `playbackRate` with `preservesPitch`; pitch uses a delay line read at
  a different rate than it is written, in an AudioWorklet, with two taps half
  a grain apart windowed by sin and cos so their squares sum to one. Either
  knob works alone.

  The chain stays connected and bypasses to unity, because rewiring a live
  graph clicks. The one exception is the shifter: it costs an audio-thread
  hop and 85 ms of delay line, so at zero semitones the signal goes around
  it, and the worklet module is not even fetched until someone asks.

Look (js/looks.js, css/aero.css)
  Nineteen visual settings — hue, second hue, saturation, surface tint,
  contrast, corner style and size, density, text size, gloss, frost, bloom,
  graph paper, backdrop scene, scene intensity, bubbles, parallax, motion,
  theme — and eight named looks that are patches over the defaults.

  All of it goes through CSS custom properties, so no component knows a
  setting exists. The three chamfer shapes are now tokens that read `--k`
  off whatever uses them, which is what lets one control turn every corner
  in the app square or round. The finished output is cached for the next
  cold start, so the head script is a replay rather than a second
  implementation of what a look looks like.

  css/aero.css is the Frutiger Aero half: an atmosphere of three enormous
  soft lights drifting behind everything, glass that actually shows the
  world through it, a specular top edge on every raised surface, and
  wireframe bubbles rising through the 3D scene. Every effect multiplies by
  --gloss, --frost or --glow; at zero the file contributes nothing.

Sound page (js/sound.js, css/sound.css)
  The response curve is measured off the filters rather than drawn from the
  slider positions, so what is on screen is what is in the signal — skirts,
  overlaps and all. Drag the curve or move the faders; both are the same
  edit. Eleven presets, saved racks, and one line of plain language saying
  what the rack is doing. B bypasses from anywhere, E opens the page.

Layout
  · The play button was a two-row grid holding two glyphs, so the visible
    one sat in the top half. Every swap button had it. They stack in one
    cell now.
  · The play triangle's bounding box was centred on 13.5 of 24, and .fab
    added a 2px nudge on top of that. Recentred in the sprite; nudge gone.
  · The sort header put its icon on the text baseline. The track row's
    number, meter and play button each found their own centre.
  · The queue pane's border made a "0px" grid column one pixel wide, so
    every route scrolled sideways by exactly one pixel at every size.
  · The transport had hard minimum columns adding to 796px and no
    breakpoint between 761 and 900, so 768 overflowed.
  · Below 560px the side rail spent 68px of a 380px window on seven icons.
    The grid re-stacks to content / transport / tabs.
  · A fixed panel parked outside the right edge counts towards the page's
    scrollable width, so the queue fades rather than slides.

tools/layout.mjs is new: eight widths × eight routes, checking overflow,
overlapping regions, unreachable controls and glyph centring. It found all
of the above. It now reports the layout clean at every width.

