From 9df9b0fcda2249670fca620fb684fc4165b65b1c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 10:37:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Keep the scroll at 60fps, and write down why
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The atmosphere behind the interface was animating, and the chrome above it is
frosted glass. Anything moving behind a backdrop-filter forces the blur to be
recomputed from scratch every frame, so a 34-second ambient drift turned a
16.7 ms scroll into 35 ms on a 3,000-track list. Pausing the animation did not
give it back — once Chromium has an animation on a full-viewport layer it
keeps paying for it whether it is running or not.

Two fixes, both found by bisecting with a measurement rather than by reasoning
about it:

  · backdrop-filter comes off every surface that scrolls. On the shelves it
    was 50 ms a frame on its own. The frost stays on the chrome, which does
    not move, and where it reads better anyway.
  · The atmosphere holds still. It looks the same in any single frame, and
    the motion in this app belongs to the wireframe world, which is drawn on
    the GPU and throttles itself.

Back to a 16.7 ms median with a 16.8 ms p95 — every frame on time — at 400
files/second import and a 7 MB heap. Both rules are now stated in aero.css
beside the code that obeys them, and in the README and the spec, because the
next person to add a frosted panel will not otherwise know.

Also in this commit:

  · tools/audio.mjs, which asks the only question that matters about a
    processing chain: does the sound change. It plays a tone and reads the
    app's own analyser, which sits after the rack. Writing it found two real
    bugs in the shifter — the ratio was read before the node existed, so
    pitch never applied, and the crossfade started before the wet path had
    anything on it, so the sound dropped out while the module was fetched —
    and two bugs in the test itself, which was measuring silence after the
    tone ended and asserting the wrong value for a keyboard nudge.
  · The pitch shifter verified independently in an OfflineAudioContext: a
    240 Hz tone comes out at 357 Hz at +7 semitones and 181 Hz at -5, with
    the level unchanged.
  · docs/SPEC.md gains F5 (the rack) and F6 (looks), with the acceptance
    criteria the suites actually assert, and the two performance rules above.
  · README covers the rack, the look panel, the new shortcuts and the
    re-measured figures.

